<h1>DIY vs. Professional Artificial Turf Cleaning: What You Can (and Can't) Do Yourself</h1>
<p>You don't need to call a professional every time your turf looks a little dusty. But there's a real line between maintenance you can handle yourself and problems that need equipment and treatments most homeowners don't have. Here's how to tell which side of that line you're on.</p>

<h2>What's safe and effective to DIY</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Regular debris removal</strong> — a leaf blower, stiff push broom, or plastic rake (never metal, which can damage fibers) to clear leaves, twigs, and loose dirt</li>
<li><strong>Spot rinsing</strong> — hosing down a specific area after a pet accident to dilute waste before it sets in</li>
<li><strong>Brushing against the grain</strong> — occasionally brushing fibers in the opposite direction they lean helps prevent matting in high-traffic paths</li>
<li><strong>Weed control at the edges</strong> — pulling any growth along borders before it gets a foothold</li>
</ul>

<h2>What consumer products usually can't fix</h2>
<p>Most store-bought "turf cleaner" sprays work on the surface only — they can knock down a smell temporarily by masking it with fragrance, but they don't reach residue that's worked its way into the infill and backing. That's the actual source of persistent pet odor, and it's why the smell often returns within a day of using a spray product.</p>

<h2>Where professional equipment makes the real difference</h2>
<p>Professional cleaning uses power brushing equipment that agitates and lifts the infill layer without damaging the backing, combined with enzymatic treatments designed specifically to break down organic waste rather than cover the smell. This is also when infill gets redistributed and topped off (see our <a href="/blog-post2">infill maintenance guide</a>) — something a garden hose and a bottle of spray simply can't do.</p>

<h2>A reasonable split</h2>
<p>Think of it as: DIY handles the surface, week to week. Professional cleaning handles what's built up below the surface, every 4–12 weeks depending on pets and usage (see our <a href="/blog-post">seasonal cleaning schedule</a>). Neither one replaces the other — yards that only get DIY maintenance tend to develop odor and matting issues within a year or two, even with regular rinsing.</p>

